dfreybur@yahoo.com says...

> Here's an attempt at a healer skill slot strategy.
> 
> The potentials are:
> 
> Expert at Healing school (4 slots), knife (5 slots),
> quarterstaff (6 slots), dart (6 slots), unihorn (6 slots)
> 
> Skilled at dagger (3), short sword (3), club (3),
> sling (3), shuriken (3)

Only the first 11 or so skill slots matter; after a certain point in the 
game, weapon skills don't affect your survival chances.

Unihorn is clearly the best melee weapon in terms of raw damage, but it 
is two-handed.  This isn't much of a drawback for a class that can't two-
weapon, but it does preclude a shield, and could lead to a real bad 
situation if it gets cursed by the titan or a castle lich.  Also, it 
isn't available for quite a while.  Good luck killing that first unicorn 
with ranged weapons (unless you find it in Sokoban).  

If circumstances dictate a one-handed weapon, elven short sword is the 
right non-artifact choice for a healer.  It does good damage (d8/d8), is 
rustproof, and can be trained up with the orc/normal/dwarf short swords 
you find before it.  I'd max it, then dart, then save darts for nasty 
monsters and use daggers at basic as the ordinary ranged weapon.

If you max unihorn skill you won't have enough slots to max darts.  You 
could ignore them in favor of daggers, or leave them at skilled and 
daggers at basic.  Or (per Jove's suggestion) only advance unihorn to 
skilled, leaving enough slots to max darts and get basic in daggers.

Putting points into Healing seems inferior to advancing weapons as fast 
as possible.


